<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  WayBionic arm - "full-arm-smaller", mechanical drop of 2026-09-05.
  AS EXPORTED. This file asserts nothing that mechanical did not supply.

  Companion to full_arm_smaller.urdf. That file is a best-effort ARTICULATED
  model and contains inferred values; this one contains none, and is the
  reference for "what did we actually receive".

  WHAT THIS FILE CONTAINS
  =======================
  Every part, at exactly the transform the export gives it, joined with FIXED
  joints. Meshes, inertials and colours are byte-for-byte the supplied values.
  The rendered result reproduces the export to within 1.2e-05 m.

  THIS ARM HAS ZERO DEGREES OF FREEDOM, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
  It cannot be planned with, and no MoveIt group can be built on it. The
  2026-09-05 export contains no kinematic information to build one from:

    - no parent/child relationships - all 12 parts hang off the assembly root
    - no joint pivot locations
    - no shoulder or elbow rotation axis (only the three coaxial tool-column
      parts carry a CAD Axis_* frame)
    - no travel, effort or velocity limits (5 sliders pinned at zero stroke,
      1 unbounded rotary)

  Producing a movable arm therefore requires GUESSING all of the above. This
  file declines to guess. Use it to check the geometry, and to show mechanical
  precisely what the delivery does and does not contain.

  THE THREE THINGS THAT ARE NOT VERBATIM
  ======================================
  1. The root aggregate mesh "Full Arm Smaller.STL" is not referenced. It is
     the entire assembly as one 218740-triangle body, and the 12 part meshes
     sum to exactly 218740 - it is the same surfaces a second time. Dropping it
     is arithmetic, not judgement: the 12 parts reproduce its bounding box to
     5.7e-05 m. Nothing is lost.
  2. Spaces in names became underscores. "3rd joint bend" would otherwise
     become a tf frame id containing spaces.
  3. base_link is a geometry-free frame placed so the assembly is centred on
     the origin in XY with its lowest point on z=0, because the STL vertices
     carry raw SolidWorks assembly coordinates ~1.5 m from the origin. This is
     presentation only - it moves every part by the same rigid transform and
     changes no relative geometry. base_link is a FRAME, not a claim about
     which component is the base.
-->
<robot name="full-arm-smaller-as-exported">

  <link name="world"/>
  <joint name="world_to_base" type="fixed">
    <parent link="world"/>
    <child link="base_link"/>
    <origin xyz="-0.726494 1.3411 -0.841727" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Frame only. The export's root link carried the duplicate aggregate mesh;
       here it carries nothing, so the 12 parts are the only geometry. -->
  <link name="base_link"/>

  <link name="stepper">
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.64313 0.8998 1.2835" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="0.45705"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.00037382" ixy="1.0743E-07" ixz="9.7233E-06" iyy="0.0005475" iyz="2.5993E-07" izz="0.00038353"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://waybionic_description/meshes/stepper.STL"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="">
        <color rgba="0.75294 0.75294 0.75294 1"/>
      </material>
    </visual>
  </link>
  <!-- Fixed: the export states where this part sits, not how it moves. -->
  <joint name="stepper_mount" type="fixed">
    <origin xyz="-0.53484 -1.9431 0.096726" rpy="1.5708 0 1.5708"/>
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="stepper"/>
  </joint>

